    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a> Muslim sultanate ruling the islands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a>, founded by tradition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-ul-hashim-of-sulu">Sharif ul-Hashim</a>.</li><li>It resisted Spanish conquest for three centuries and grew rich exporting sea produce to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.</li><li>Its 1878 grant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> to a British syndicate is the origin of the Philippine claim to Sabah.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paduka-batara">Paduka Batara</a>, a Sulu ruler who died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1417, is buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dezhou">Dezhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>; members of his family stayed to tend the tomb and their descendants still live there.</p></aside>
    </section>

