    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paduka-batara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paduka-batara.jpg" alt="Paduka Batara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paduka-batara">Paduka Batara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%BE%B7%E5%B7%9E%E8%8B%8F%E7%A6%84%E7%8E%8B%E5%A2%93.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Suginami, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sulu Sultanate grew out of the trading chiefdoms of the Sulu archipelago, the chain of islands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a> and Sama peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> probably reached the islands with Muslim traders and teachers in the fourteenth century; Sulu tradition remembers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-ul-makhdum">Karim ul-Makhdum</a>, credited with building a mosque on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simunul">Simunul</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawi-tawi">Tawi-Tawi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-baguinda">Raja Baguinda</a>, a prince said to have come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minangkabau-people">Minangkabau</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. Chinese records show that in 1417 three Sulu rulers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paduka-batara">Paduka Batara</a>, led a large mission to the Ming court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a>; Paduka Batara died on the return journey and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dezhou">Dezhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, where his tomb still stands. The sultanate itself is traditionally credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-ul-hashim-of-sulu">Sharif ul-Hashim</a>, also called Sayyid Abu Bakr, an Arab scholar said in the Sulu genealogies to have come by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, who married Baguinda's daughter and took the title of sultan. The date is uncertain: the genealogies (<em>tarsila</em>) and later chronologies place it anywhere between 1405 and the mid-fifteenth century. Sharif ul-Hashim is remembered as a lawgiver who introduced Islamic institutions and divided the islands into districts under chiefs. The court settled first at Buansa and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a>, which became its lasting capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tausug"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tausug.jpg" alt="Tausug" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tausug.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jjarivera, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a> society was organised around the sultan, the hereditary datus who led districts and followings of their own, free commoners, and dependants and slaves. Sama sea-going communities and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranun">Iranun</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> coast stood in looser relationships to the court. The economy rested on the sea. Sulu divers harvested pearls and mother-of-pearl, and the islands gathered tripang (sea cucumber), edible birds' nests from the caves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, tortoiseshell and wax, products prized above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. From the 1760s British country traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, followed by Chinese junks and other Western merchants, exchanged opium, cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and especially firearms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> for these goods, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> became a busy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a>. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-warren">James Francis Warren</a> has shown that this boom created a vast demand for labour, met by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a>: Iranun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balangingi-sama">Balangingi Sama</a> fleets based in the Sulu zone seized, by his estimates, thousands of captives a year from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> and the Malay world, many of whom were set to fishing and collecting marine produce or sold onward. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, practised alongside customary law and older local beliefs, gave the sultanate its identity, and religious teachers and pilgrims linked Jolo to the wider Muslim world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-bud-dajo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-bud-dajo.jpg" alt="First Battle of Bud Dajo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-bud-dajo">First Battle of Bud Dajo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bud_Dajo_Massacre_Trench_on_Jolo_Island_c1906.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pershing, John J. (John Joseph), 1860-1948., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sulu had no standing army; the sultan relied on the armed followings of the datus, who fought with the <em>kris</em>, the <em>barong</em> and spears, and increasingly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and <em>lantaka</em>, small bronze swivel guns cast locally or bought from traders. Defence rested on fortified <em>kota</em> of stone, coral and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and on reefs and shallows that hampered large ships. The Spanish, established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> from 1571, launched repeated expeditions in the long conflicts they called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moro-people">Moro</a> wars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> was attacked as early as 1578, and in 1638 Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-hurtado-de-corcuera">Sebastián Hurtado de Corcuera</a> captured it after a siege of several months; the Spanish garrison was withdrawn in 1646. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> abandoned its fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a> in 1662 and did not return until 1718, giving Sulu a long respite. Sulu and allied fleets in turn raided Spanish-held coasts, and in 1775 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a> warriors destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a>'s new post on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balambangan-island">Balambangan Island</a>. The raiders lost their edge only when steam gunboats arrived: Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narciso-claveria">Narciso Clavería</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balangingi-sama">Balangingi</a> strongholds in 1848, Jolo was stormed in 1851, and in 1876 a large expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-malcampo">José Malcampo</a> occupied it. Under American rule, armed resistance ended with the massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-bud-dajo">Bud Dajo</a> in 1906 and defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bud-bagsak">Bud Bagsak</a> in 1913.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sharif-ul-hashim-of-sulu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sharif-ul-hashim-of-sulu.jpg" alt="Sharif ul-Hashim of Sulu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sharif-ul-hashim-of-sulu">Sharif ul-Hashim of Sulu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sayyid_Abubakr_bin_Shaikh_Al-Kaff_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultan stood at the head of a Muslim state but ruled more through consensus than command. He was chosen from the royal lineage descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-ul-hashim-of-sulu">Sharif ul-Hashim</a>, and his authority over the datus, who controlled their own districts, trade and fighting men, depended on wealth, kinship and religious prestige. A council of leading datus and officials, the <em>ruma bichara</em>, advised on succession, war and treaties, and disputes over the throne between rival branches of the dynasty were frequent. Islamic law, interpreted by religious officials, coexisted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a> customary law (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a></em>). The court controlled trade at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a>, levying duties and dealing with foreign merchants, which gave sultans the means to reward followers. Sulu's reach extended through tributary ties to coastal chiefs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palawan">Palawan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilan">Basilan</a> and northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>; according to Sulu tradition, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Brunei granted it the lands of northern Borneo in return for help in Brunei's civil war of 1660–1673. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azim-ud-din-i-of-sulu">Azim ud-Din I</a>, seeking Spanish help against his brother Bantilan, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, was baptised in 1750 and was later imprisoned, an episode that divided the court. In the nineteenth century diplomacy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> became as important to survival as warfare.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dent"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-dent.jpg" alt="Alfred Dent" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dent">Alfred Dent</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlfredDent.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sulu was one of the most durable Muslim states of maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and, with the Maguindanao Sultanate, the main reason <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> never controlled the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Three centuries of resistance shaped the distinct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moro-people">Moro</a> identity of the Muslim peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and Sulu, which remains central to Philippine politics and lay behind the struggle for autonomy that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangsamoro">Bangsamoro</a> autonomous region in 2019. The sultanate's commercial role made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> a node linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the Malay world, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and the Spanish Philippines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-warren">Warren</a>'s work on the 'Sulu zone' has made it a classic case of how global trade could reshape a regional state and drive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The raids of the allied fleets left deep marks on the coastal societies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, where watchtowers and fortified churches were built against them. The most lasting legacy is legal and diplomatic. The 1878 agreement under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamalul-alam">Jamalul Alam</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-von-overbeck">Baron Gustav von Overbeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dent">Alfred Dent</a> underlies the Philippine claim to Sabah, formally raised in 1962, and the claims of the sultan's heirs, which remain a source of friction between the Philippines and Malaysia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/narciso-claveria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narciso-claveria.jpg" alt="Narciso Clavería" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/narciso-claveria">Narciso Clavería</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_teniente_general_Narciso_Claver%C3%ADa_y_Zald%C3%BAa,_conde_de_Manila_(Museo_del_Prado).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sulu's decline came with the steam gunboat and the colonial partition of the region. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narciso-claveria">Clavería</a>'s campaign of 1848 and the storming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> in 1851, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-pulalun">Muhammad Pulalun</a> signed a treaty acknowledging Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, though Sulu continued in practice to act independently. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s occupation of Jolo in 1876 forced the court inland, and in 1878 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamalul-alam">Jamalul Alam</a> accepted Spanish protection. In the same year he signed the grant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a>, read by one side as a lease and by the other as a cession, in exchange for an annual payment; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-north-borneo-company">British North Borneo Company</a> received its charter in 1881, and the Madrid Protocol of 1885 confirmed Spanish sovereignty over the archipelago while Spain renounced its claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. After Spain ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1898, General John C. Bates negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiram-bates-treaty">Bates Treaty</a> of 1899 with Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamalul-kiram-ii">Jamalul Kiram II</a>, recognising his position under American sovereignty; the United States abrogated it in 1904 and governed Sulu directly through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moro-people">Moro</a> Province. In 1915, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpenter-agreement">Carpenter Agreement</a>, Jamalul Kiram II renounced temporal power while remaining religious head of Sulu's Muslims. The title passed after his death in 1936 to rival claimants, and the sultanate's former lands now lie within the Philippines and Malaysia.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sulu Sultanate, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a> Muslim state ruling the islands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> from the fifteenth century to 1915, resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> for three centuries, grew rich on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> trade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> of the Sulu zone, and left behind the unresolved Philippine claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>.</em></p>

